@RPT
Feature: The report renders the ledger as sectioned facts

  The report's FACTS are generated by the renderer, so no hand-authored narrative wraps the
  output.

  The run's provenance (target, inputs, run start and source SHA) travels from the CLI to the
  renderers beside the ledger, which stays pure. The markdown renderer is SECTIONED: an
  ordered list of section emitters — title, provenance, the summary views, the run verdict,
  the ledger and the rest — each over the one assembled report model.

  Facts only — no interpretation, editorial, or cross-run comparison (project
  design §5).

  Rule: the markdown report opens with a run-provenance header

    The headline behaviour (foundation-bearing). The markdown report no
    longer opens straight into the per-scenario table: it opens with a "## Run
    provenance" section stating the target repo, the input paths/flags actually
    used, the run-start timestamp in UTC, the target's source SHA (a null SHA
    rendered as an explicit "unknown (non-git target)"), and the TOOL's own SHA and
    version — which spec-controller produced the run, a null tool SHA
    rendered "unknown (not a git checkout)" with the version still shown.

    The "Target repo:" fact is sourced from --target VERBATIM — no resolution,
    defaulting to "unknown-target" on omit, parallel to sourceSha's "unknown"
    (Fork A; the facts artefact is self-describing, run.yaml optional in
    CI). The prior basename(dirname(--features)) resolution — and its degenerate "."
    — are gone: the target is now the operator's --target value or the explicit
    default, never a guess.

    @unit @SCN-RPT-001
    Scenario: the markdown report opens with a Run provenance header whose Target repo is the --target value verbatim, defaulting to unknown-target

  Rule: the JSON report carries a provenance object

    The machine-readable surface. The JSON report grows a
    top-level `provenance` object carrying the same non-derivable run facts as the
    markdown header — target, source SHA, the TOOL's own SHA and version (which
    spec-controller produced the run), the UTC run timestamp, and the input
    paths/flags actually used — while the reconciliation body (scenarios, warnings)
    is unchanged: the serialised shape becomes { provenance, scenarios, warnings }.
    Provenance is not derivable from the Ledger, so it is persisted on the
    JSON surface (project design §1, §3 — persist a fact on JSON iff it is not
    derivable from the balance; the derived human views stay off JSON).

    @unit @SCN-RPT-002
    Scenario: the JSON report carries a provenance object stating target, source SHA, tool SHA, tool version, UTC timestamp, and inputs, with the reconciliation body intact

  Rule: an explicit source SHA wins, and a non-git target resolves to unknown

    The resolution rule. The source SHA on the report's
    provenance is resolved by one rule: an explicit
    non-empty source SHA — the operator-supplied override flag — wins over any git
    read, so an auditor reconciling a zip export, a detached-HEAD checkout, or a tree
    with no .git can still outcome a complete provenance. When no override is given and
    the target is not a git repository, the git read cannot answer and the source SHA
    resolves to a recorded null ("unknown") rather than crashing the run (project
    design §4). A pure resolution rule, so it is proven in isolation by Vitest, over a controlled non-git temp directory.

    @unit @SCN-RPT-003
    Scenario: an explicit source SHA overrides the git read, and a non-git target with no override resolves to unknown

  Rule: the markdown report renders the evidence reconciliation as a subjects-rows table with balanced and out-of-balance columns

    The evidence reconciliation, as subjects-rows. Under "## Summary views", a
    "### Evidence reconciliation" section renders ONE table whose SUBJECTS are rows — Scenario
    Reconciliation and Quality Check Reconciliation — each with its own Total, and balanced /
    out-of-balance as COLUMNS (both subjects report into the same binary, so the
    "Axis"/"Verdict"/"outcome" words retire). The second row counts every Quality Check
    Reconciliation on every row — phantom and suspense included, and an Evidence Observation
    that answers no Evidence Obligation included — so it is not a count of Evidence
    Obligations. The Quality Check Reconciliation out-of-balance is decomposed in place into its
    reconciling-item causes: three nested ("↳") rows in canonical order — missing,
    failing, and the watermelon watermelon —
    populating the out-of-balance column only, always shown (a zero cause is itself a fact).
    The missing row carries the bare-level split as its Detail (a behavioural
    quality check by its bare level, a fitness-function one as "fitness" — the label-=-tag lock),
    canonical order (fitness first), counting missing Quality Check Reconciliations by quality
    check, not scenarios. The Scenario Reconciliation and Quality Check Reconciliation
    out-of-balance counts diverge on purpose — a scenario with two missing arms is one
    out-of-balance Scenario Reconciliation and two out-of-balance Quality Check
    Reconciliations — and the table presents that as a granularity fact, the nested causes
    reconciling to the Quality Check Reconciliation out-of-balance. No bold. A pure derive over
    balance.scenarioReconciliations[].qualityCheckReconciliations[] — markdown-only (project
    design §3), proven in isolation by Vitest.

    The subject row is the SCENARIO RECONCILIATION — the Evidence Reconciliation's collection, all
    three row kinds (real, phantom, suspense) — not "Scenario", which names only the
    real thing. The table is a pure presentation of summary.scenarioReconciliations, whose
    totals count EVERY row (phantom / suspense reconciliations are
    always out-of-balance), so on a run whose only out-of-balance row is the suspense
    reconciliation the table renders N+1 | N | 1 — never N | N | 0. The subject's
    out-of-balance column therefore agrees with the balance verdict and with the exit code:
    three views of one fact, which must never disagree.

    @unit @SCN-RPT-004
    Scenario: the markdown report renders the evidence reconciliation as subjects-rows with balanced and out-of-balance columns

  Rule: the markdown report surfaces the fitness evidence obligations as their own section

    The by-quality-check view (under Summary views with a total
    line; labelled by the quality check, fitness, never an internal kind).
    The fitness evidence obligations sit mixed in with behavioural items in the
    per-scenario ledger. This section lifts them out: under "## Summary views", a
    "### Fitness evidence obligations" section opens with a total line ("N of M fitness
    evidence obligations reconcile satisfied.") then a table listing each fitness evidence
    obligation by its scenario and its reconciliation outcome — so the fitness axis (incl. any
    🍉 watermelon) reads on its own. Omitted entirely (total line and all) when no scenario
    raises a fitness evidence obligation. A pure derive over the ledger's fitness sub-rows —
    markdown-only (project design §3), proven in isolation by Vitest.

    NO CHECK COLUMN. The table once printed a "Static check" column carrying the
    characteristic the obligation was keyed by — what the check was ABOUT. One @fitness scenario
    is one check and what it is about is its scenario's title, so no report rolls a check up by a
    word — a fitness function is its scenario; the scenario's @SCN is
    the whole identity the row needs.

    @unit @SCN-RPT-005
    Scenario: the markdown report lists the fitness evidence obligations as their own section

  Rule: the markdown report groups out-of-balance scenarios by feature

    The by-feature view (under Summary views with a total
    line and rendered as a TABLE; the FFF prefix enriched to the
    full feature name). The per-scenario ledger lists every scenario in document
    order; it does not show which FEATURES carry the discrepancies. Under "## Summary
    views", a "### Out-of-balance by feature" section opens with a total line ("N of M
    scenario rows out of balance, grouped by feature.") then a "Feature | Count | Out-of-balance
    ids" table grouping the verdict === "out-of-balance" scenarios under the <FFF> parsed
    from each scnId (SCN-<FFF>-<NNN>), one row per feature with a per-feature count,
    so a reader sees the discrepancy CONCENTRATION by feature. The Feature cell reads
    "<full-name> (<FFF>)" — the full name is the feature file's filename slug, looked up via
    a target-derived code→slug map, read off the discovered feature files and carried on the
    report model's metadata. A FOREIGN-SCN-CODE id (an @SCN-XYZ-* whose XYZ no file carries)
    falls back to the bare FFF (the map misses) — as does the no-map back-compat path.
    Balanced scenarios are excluded (this is the discrepancy view). The scnId → <FFF> parse
    REUSES the one shared feature-code parse rather than a fresh regex. A pure derive over
    the ledger rows + the feature-title lookup. Vitest proves the render (full name + foreign-scn-code fallback) in isolation.

    THE TOTAL LINE ACCOUNTS FOR EVERY OUT-OF-BALANCE ROW. Only scenario rows are
    grouped — a phantom-scenario or suspense-scenario row names no feature file, so it has no
    group to sit in. But the sentence is what a reader arbitrating a deletion reads, and a
    stranded citation is exactly what a deletion leaves: a feature file gone while evidence
    still cites its scenarios. So the sentence names each out-of-balance phantom-scenario and
    suspense-scenario count as not grouped by feature, and its numbers add up to the header's
    out-of-balance and total. The section is omitted only when no Scenario Reconciliation of
    any kind is out of balance; when only ungrouped rows are, it states them and has no table.

    A SCENARIO CARRYING NO @SCN CODE has no <FFF> to parse, and it still belongs to a feature: it
    counts on the row of the feature file it sits in, under that file's header code when the file
    carries one, and under the file's name when it carries none. Its cell states how many such
    scenarios the row holds rather than naming them, because the column claims @SCN ids and they
    have none; the untagged-scenario list names each one where it lives. The file and its header
    code are read from the report itself, so a re-render from the saved JSON groups them the same.

    @unit @SCN-RPT-006
    Scenario: the by-feature cut names each out-of-balance feature in full, falling back to the bare code for a foreign-scn-code

    @unit @SCN-RPT-030
    Scenario: the by-feature total line counts the out-of-balance rows no feature can group, even when they are the only ones

    @unit @SCN-RPT-031
    Scenario: a feature file's scenarios carrying no @SCN code count together on one row named by that file, never one row each

    @unit @SCN-RPT-032
    Scenario: a scenario carrying no @SCN code in a file headed by a feature code counts on that feature's row

  Rule: the corpus census survives its own section's dissolution

    The corpus census (no standalone census section). The count is NOT lost: EVERY scenario is a ledger row — a
    zero-obligation scenario is a both-missing row, not a second axis — so the census
    is recoverable as the ledger scenario-count ("- N Scenario Reconciliation(s): …")
    alone. The dissolution is guarded POSITIVELY, over the report's whole set of
    top-level sections: a census section that came back under ANY name reds
    by being unexpected, where naming one retired heading guarded only that spelling.
    A pure markdown-only derive over the balance (project design §3), proven in
    isolation by Vitest. RPT-008's excluded-occurrence bullet relocates into the
    ledger summary.

    @unit @SCN-RPT-007
    Scenario: the corpus census is recoverable from the ledger summary alone

  Rule: the report outcomes the excluded non-tag @SCN occurrences on both surfaces

    The corpus is reconciled against every raw mention. The report outcomes "N @SCN ids seen in non-tag context, excluded" — the
    delta between raw @SCN-FFF-NNN occurrences in the source (prose, comments, step
    text) and the ids actually read as scenarios — on BOTH surfaces (the
    markdown ledger-summary bullet + a JSON evidenceObligations object). Corpus metadata NOT
    derivable from the Ledger, so it lands on both surfaces (project design §1, §3 — the same
    non-derivable rule that put provenance on both surfaces). The scenario parse counts the
    raw occurrences beside the scenarios it reads; the datum is a field of the
    evidence-obligations census, threaded from the CLI to the renderers like the run's
    provenance.

    @unit @SCN-RPT-008
    Scenario: the report outcomes the excluded non-tag @SCN occurrences on both surfaces

  Rule: the report renders summary-first, ledger-last

    The report layout.
    The report opens with "# Evidence Reconciliation" (the report IS the reconciliation; "Ledger"
    names only the INPUT, so the reconciled OUTPUT is never titled "Ledger"), keeps
    "## Run provenance" first, then groups the derived cuts under "## Summary views"
    (### Run verdict → ### Evidence reconciliation → ### Test volume → ### Fitness functions →
    "## Scenario Reconciliations" table to the END with its
    summary lines on top (the verdict tally and the relocated excluded-occurrence bullet),
    "## Warnings" last. So the digestible summary leads and the 500-row ledger trails, and
    the cuts read as cuts OF the ledger, not a separate ledger. A pure layout reorg
    over existing data — no new derivation, the JSON surface unchanged (markdown-only).
    The RPT-004/005/006/007 scenarios are updated in the same change. Every reconciling
    item is labelled by its TAG (the bare level for behavioural — unit / integration / … —
    and "fitness" for the fitness axis), never the internal kind axis. Proven in isolation by Vitest.

    @unit @SCN-RPT-009
    Scenario: the markdown report renders summary-first with the scenario reconciliations last

  Rule: the report reconciles the test evidence observation — raw = attributed + suspense

    The evidence observation-reconciliation spine. The report's evidence obligation side has a
    Headline tally, and its evidence observation side has its own: the observed test evidence
    observations are counted by kind against the @SCN anchor in ONE shared pass — on a scenario,
    on a phantom scenario (an @SCN no scenario carries), and on the suspense scenario (no @SCN)
    — with raw per-level totals and file locators. Under "## Summary views", a
    "### Test volume" cut renders the balancing identity ("N evidence observations = a on scenarios +
    b on phantom scenarios + c on the suspense scenario", a derived view) then a by-level evidence observation table (all four levels,
    e2e/performance zeros shown — a fixed tally that always lists every level). The identity holds
    BY CONSTRUCTION: one pass counts each evidence observation into exactly one kind while
    summing the raw total, so the three terms can't drift. The non-derivable atoms
    (rawByBehaviouralQualityCheck, onScenario, onPhantomScenario, onSuspenseScenario) persist on JSON (project design §3); the
    identity line stays markdown-only.
    Foundational: later scenarios consume the aggregate.

    @unit @SCN-RPT-010
    Scenario: the report renders the Test volume cut and the evidence observation identity balances by construction

  Rule: the per-scenario ledger shows the evidence backing each evidence obligation

    Per-scenario evidence columns, consuming the evidence observation
    aggregate. The ledger row gains corpus-driven columns between Scenario and Verdict —
    Fitness first, then the behavioural levels present in evidence obligation or evidence observation (Unit, Integration,
    e2e, Performance; e2e/performance only when a run has them). Each cell is two-sided,
    `<obligation>/<observation>`: the LEFT is the evidence obligation — a single quality-check letter (F, U, I,
    E, P) iff the scenario obligated this check, else `-`; the RIGHT is the observation count — the
    multiplicity of posted evidence observations at this check (backing present, pass OR fail), else `-`. So a
    named-and-met cell reads `letter/N`; a named-and-missing cell reads `letter/-`; a
    NO-EVIDENCE-OBLIGATION observation (an evidence observation at a quality-check the scenario never named) reads `-/N`;
    a doubly-empty cell (no evidence obligation, no evidence observation) renders BLANK.
    The cell counts BACKING, not quality — a failing evidence obligation shows its
    count, its failing-ness staying in the Verdict + Reconciling-items columns.
    The Fitness cell reads the fitness evidence obligation outcomes (a fitness evidence observation isn't counted):
    its right side is the count of guards present (balanced or the watermelon — the script exists; its
    inadequacy is the 🍉 in Reconciling-items); its left is the single `F` — one scenario is one
    fitness function, so a row names at most one fitness function. No Cell/Meaning legend
    precedes the table — the grid speaks for itself. Markdown-only — the observation atoms are
    already on JSON. The "count is backing, not
    quality — never rank by it" discipline is a documented rule, NOT rendered.

    The no-evidence-obligation cell (`-/N`) posts from ONE production shape: the reconciler posts
    an evidence-observation row ON the real scenario, outcome `no-evidence-obligation`, and that row carries the observation
    on its `evidenceObservations`. The cell reads its right-side count STRAIGHT off those posted
    evidence observations (multiplicity-correct) — never off `qualityCheckReconciliations`, which
    collapse to one matched row per obligation and would undercount duplicate citations. An
    `no-evidence-obligation` row is NOT an evidence obligation: its left side is `-`, so the cell cannot be mistaken for a
    named-and-missing one (`letter/-`), which points at the OPPOSITE remedy — "write the test",
    when the truth is "fix the attribution". A pending obligation (@unitpending) names nothing —
    the pending pair is folded out of the active set — so an evidence observation citing it reconciles the same
    NO-EVIDENCE-OBLIGATION way and renders `-/N` too. Proven in isolation by Vitest over a RECONCILER-TRUE
    fixture (the balance is produced by `reconcile()` over a scenario + observation corpus, never
    hand-written, so a fixture cannot pin a shape the pipeline cannot emit).

    @unit @SCN-RPT-011
    Scenario: the ledger row shows corpus-driven evidence columns with the two-sided cell encoding

  Rule: Warnings flags the untagged scenarios with their feature locators

    Anchor audit — untagged scenarios, the obligation-side
    counterpart to the evidence observation anomalies. The scenario parse emits only
    scenarios WITH an @SCN; the untagged count takes the Gherkin scenario
    headers (Scenario / Scenario Outline / Scenario Template / Example — NOT the
    Examples: data table) and subtracts those whose preceding tag block carries an @SCN.
    The ledger-summary bullets list the untagged-scenario count + `feature-file : scenario`
    locators (sorted). The datum lives on the evidence-obligations census's untaggedScenarios
    (obligation-side, like excludedOccurrences) and — a non-derivable datum — persists on the
    JSON evidenceObligations object beside excludedOccurrences (project design §3). Facts-only
    — count + locator.

    @unit @SCN-RPT-014
    Scenario: Warnings lists the untagged scenarios with their feature locators

  Rule: a cross-repo reconcile relativises paths against the target repo root, not the run cwd

    Portability. Provenance inputs and evidence-observation locators are relativised against
    the TARGET's root, never the run cwd. Reconciling a DIFFERENT repo is the primary use case,
    and there the target's absolute paths are NOT under cwd, so relativising against cwd would
    keep them verbatim and leak the local FS layout: absolute /Users/… paths across
    provenance.inputs and the phantom and suspense evidence-observation locators. A stored
    report is more sensitive than code and public-bound, so such a leak defeats the
    portability thesis.

    The root is derived from the target: the git top-level of dirname(--features) when the
    target is a git repo, else dirname(--features) itself, else process.cwd() for a
    self-reconcile with no --features. That single derived root is threaded at the CLI edge to
    BOTH the provenance-input relativisation and the evidence-observation locator
    relativisation, so output is portable regardless of where the tool is invoked.

    The unit arm proves the target repo root is derived from --features, independent of the
    run cwd. The integration arm drives the REAL CLI, run from a cwd
    that is NOT the target repo, over a target whose Vitest / Cucumber reports carry
    ABSOLUTE test-file locators: it persists a markdown + JSON report carrying ZERO of the
    target's absolute paths — the evidence-observation locators collapse to repo-relative
    and the input paths record relative to the target root. Relativising against the run cwd
    instead reds it, because the target's paths leak verbatim.

    @unit @integration @SCN-RPT-016
    Scenario: a cross-repo reconcile run from a foreign cwd yields zero absolute paths on both surfaces
      Given a target repo outside the run's working directory whose Vitest and Cucumber reports carry absolute test-file locators under that repo
      When balance reconciles it from a foreign working directory and saves the reconciliation in markdown and JSON
      Then the saved reconciliation, in both markdown and JSON, carries none of the target's absolute paths — the evidence-observation locators are repo-relative and the input paths record relative to the target root

  Rule: the report's evidence-observation views level a behavioural observation by the cited scenario's tag

    The RECONCILER levels a behavioural observation by the cited scenario's own tier tag, so a
    cucumber run of an @e2e scenario discharges an e2e obligation. The report's
    evidence-observation views — Test volume's `rawByBehaviouralQualityCheck` and the per-scenario
    E2e cell — must say the same, or the report contradicts itself: an @e2e-via-cucumber
    observation surfacing as `e2e: 0` in the views while the reconciler reports the e2e
    obligation satisfied.

    So the counting is a FOLD OVER THE LEDGER'S POSTED EVIDENCE OBSERVATIONS. It walks the evidence observations on each
    Scenario Reconciliation row, taking each evidence observation's LEVEL off the evidence observation the parse emitted
    and its ACCOUNT off the row it posted to — the reconciler's own ruling, re-derived nowhere.
    There is no second walk to disagree with the first: the one parsed set of evidence
    observations (tag-derived, per-tier) is what reconcile posts, and the fold counts what was
    posted, so the level the parse derived IS the level the views render. The E2e cell likewise
    reads its observation count STRAIGHT off those posted evidence observations, never off a parallel tally.

    The fold carries `rawByBehaviouralQualityCheck` beside three per-account counts — `onScenario`
    (both the matched and the no-evidence-obligation evidence observations), `onPhantomScenario` and
    `onSuspenseScenario` — and the outcome split per evidence observation lives on the ledger rows themselves,
    where the reconciler ruled it. The completeness identity is
    `Σ raw = onScenario + onPhantomScenario + onSuspenseScenario`, holding by construction over
    the single walk.

    The unit arm proves the fold: over a reconciler-built spanning corpus carrying an
    e2e-levelled evidence observation, `rawByBehaviouralQualityCheck` counts it under e2e and not integration.

    @unit @SCN-RPT-017
    Scenario: an @e2e-via-cucumber observation is counted at e2e across the report views

    @unit @SCN-RPT-018
    Scenario: an @integration @e2e-via-cucumber observation is counted in both tiers across the views

  Rule: the Quality Check Reconciliation out-of-balance decomposes into a flat list of the ten leaf outcomes, with no group-side subtotal rows

    The Quality Check Reconciliation summary decomposition. The Evidence
    reconciliation's Quality Check Reconciliation out-of-balance row decomposes into a FLAT list of the
    ten out-of-balance leaf outcomes — missing, failing, watermelon,
    no-scn-code, duplicate-scn-code, cites-unknown-scenario, cites-no-scenario, no-evidence-obligation, broken, both-missing — each a counted
    "↳ <outcome>" row whose Out-of-balance counts SUM to the Quality Check Reconciliation out-of-balance
    total. The former debit/credit/neither grouping is removed entirely: no "↳ debit-side",
    "↳ credit-side", or "↳ neither-side" subtotal row is rendered. The grouping construct
    double-counted — each group emitted a subtotal row AND its leaf rows at the same indent, so
    a naive column-sum doubled (170 vs 85). Removing the grouping leaves the leaves reconciling
    to the total exactly once. The existing leaf-presence guard stays green (the
    leaves still render). A pure derive over the report's materialised
    summary.qualityCheckReconciliations.byOutcome — markdown-only, proven in isolation by Vitest.

    The leaf list is HAND-MAINTAINED (a readonly Outcome[], not a type-forced Record), so it can
    fall behind the Outcome union silently — broken joined the union, tallied in
    the JSON via byOutcome.broken, and would have been omitted from this table with a clean
    compile. So the decomposition also carries an EXHAUSTIVENESS guard: every Outcome member
    except satisfied (the balanced outcome) renders a leaf row — derived from the union itself,
    never a hand-transcribed list, so a future member cannot join the union without reaching the
    table. It WORKED: `no-scn-code` could not be added to the union without
    this decomposition acknowledging it — the compiler refused the type-forced Record until it did.
    The ninth leaf therefore reached the table in the same commit as the outcome, by construction.
    The tenth, `duplicate-scn-code`, is the same move again: a debit-side outcome, so it
    reads after `no-scn-code`, the other debit-side outcome.

    @unit @SCN-RPT-019
    Scenario: the Quality Check Reconciliation summary lists the ten leaf outcomes summing to the out-of-balance total, with no group rows

  Rule: the markdown renders each outcome's sentence in place of the bare handle

    The outcome gloss. A reconciling-item outcome handle
    (missing, cites-no-scenario, no-evidence-obligation, both-missing, …) is meaningless to a human
    reading the report in a CI log. So the markdown renders each outcome's full
    SENTENCE in place of the handle, EVERYWHERE an outcome surfaces to a reader: the
    Evidence-reconciliation summary's "↳" decomposition rows (Subject cell), the per-scenario
    reconciling-items list, and the fitness-obligation table's Outcome column. "↳ missing"
    becomes "↳ Evidence Observation is missing"; "unit ∅ missing" becomes "unit ∅ Evidence
    Observation is missing"; "🍉 watermelon" becomes "🍉 Evidence Observation is passing but
    its check could not fail its job". The handle is GONE from the markdown; the glyph is retained. The descriptions
    are SINGLE-SOURCED from the one table of outcome descriptions, next to the outcome type, so
    the render cannot drift from the outcome it names. Markdown ONLY: the JSON keeps the bare handle (the machine axis; a
    programmatic consumer uses it). The Detail column is unchanged. A pure derive over the
    report model, proven in isolation by Vitest.

    @unit @SCN-RPT-020
    Scenario: the markdown renders each outcome's sentence, not the bare handle

  Rule: the gloss for every outcome is its sentence, verbatim

    The single owner of the outcome gloss text. OUTCOME_DESCRIPTION is what the renderer shows
    in place of the bare handle everywhere an outcome surfaces to a reader, so its text IS the report's
    gloss. This pins every handle to its outcome sentence, verbatim, keyed by the type-forced
    Outcome union so a new outcome cannot ship without its gloss. (The run-integrity stamp and
    verdict line are governed by the Unsound entry and stay reader-agnostic, not this Rule's
    concern. The Rule above holds that the markdown renders this text in place of the handle;
    this Rule holds that the text is the outcome's sentence.)

    @unit @SCN-RPT-025
    Scenario Outline: the <handle> outcome glosses as its sentence, verbatim

      Examples:
        | handle                 | sentence                                                                                                                 |
        | satisfied              | Evidence Observation satisfies its Evidence Obligation                                                                    |
        | missing                | Evidence Observation is missing                                                                                          |
        | failing                | Evidence Observation is failing                                                                                          |
        | watermelon             | Evidence Observation is passing but its check could not fail its job                                                      |
        | no-scn-code            | Evidence Obligation has no @SCN code for an Evidence Observation to link to                                               |
        | duplicate-scn-code     | Evidence Obligation's @SCN code is carried by another Scenario                                                            |
        | cites-unknown-scenario | Evidence Observation cites an unknown Scenario                                                                            |
        | cites-no-scenario      | Evidence Observation cites no Scenario                                                                                    |
        | no-evidence-obligation             | Evidence Observation cites a Scenario with no matching Evidence Obligation                                                |
        | broken                 | Evidence Observation ATTEMPT errored before its proof ran (behaviour never exercised)                                     |
        | both-missing           | Evidence Obligation and Evidence Observation are both missing — the Scenario is untagged and uncited                     |

  Rule: the report surfaces broken evidence as a harness failure, and stamps the run unsound

    The customer-visible payoff of the broken reconciling item, over vitest and
    cucumber alike. A "missing" row tells the reader to WRITE A TEST. When the proof
    already exists and simply NEVER RAN — a vitest suite that failed to collect, a cucumber step
    two step definitions both match — that instruction is wrong, and the report is the only place
    the reader will ever see it. So the markdown does three things a "missing" row cannot.

    It NAMES the broken evidence — the locator the runner gave it, a test file or a feature file —
    and PRINTS what broke, so the reader knows what and where. The message is printed VERBATIM in
    the run-integrity section (a fenced block — a vitest collection message is multi-line and would
    destroy a table cell); the suspense row's "broken" reconciling item carries the same locator
    plus the message's headline line, so the row is legible without leaving the table.

    The renderer needs NO per-reader branch — it renders what the item CARRIES. Where the runner
    gives its own words (vitest's collection error) the message IS those words, passed through
    unchanged; where the runner gives none (cucumber emits no error_message for an ambiguous step)
    the reader synthesised the diagnosis upstream. Either way the renderer names WHAT failed and
    WHERE, in one shape — and it never miscalls one kind of break as the other.

    It STAMPS the run "unsound" — the render headline word, and the only place that word appears
    (the predicate asks whether any evidence is broken; the word is the reader's). The stamp is what tells the reader that every "missing" outcome BELOW it is
    suspect: an unexercised behaviour reconciles missing whether its proof is absent or merely
    broken, so a "missing" row in an unsound run cannot be read as "write the test".

    The gloss is REMEDY-NEUTRAL: what broke is equally a missing dependency, a syntax error, a bad
    import, a top-level throw, or two step definitions colliding, so the report names the fact and
    prints the message it was given — it never prescribes a cure. The one remedy it does give is
    the one that always holds: fix the harness and regenerate.

    Omit-when-empty (the Pending Items tally precedent): a run carrying no broken evidence
    renders no run-integrity section — a sound run gains no stray empty section.
    Markdown ONLY — a machine consumer reads soundness as summary.byOutcome.broken > 0, already
    type-forced into the JSON, so no new JSON field is owed. A pure derive over the report model,
    proven in isolation by Vitest.

    @unit @SCN-RPT-021
    Scenario: the markdown names the broken evidence and its error, and stamps the run unsound

  Rule: the summary opens with the run's verdict

    The tally fails on its own (every Scenario Reconciliation is
    counted, so a suspense row makes the totals read out-of-balance), but a reader still has to
    READ it. The verdict is the bottom line, so the summary says it OUTRIGHT — one line that IS
    the outcome, coherent with the "## Run integrity: unsound" stamp and the
    Pending Items line. A run that exits non-zero can then never render a headline that reads
    as a pass: the summary's FIRST words are the failure.

    The verdict is read off the WHOLE-RUN predicates — broken evidence, balance, pending
    items — never re-scanned by the renderer, and in the CLI's own dominance order
    (broken → out-of-balance → pending). Asking the same predicates in the
    same order is what stops the headline drifting from the exit code it names.

    UNSOUND maps to exit 4, OUT-OF-BALANCE to exit 1, BALANCED to exit 0 — determinate from
    the reconciliation alone. Exit 3 is NOT: it fires only under --strict, a CLI-edge flag the
    core cannot see, so a balanced run carrying Pending Items names exit 3 CONDITIONALLY
    ("exit 3 under --strict") and never asserts it. Unlike the unsound stamp, this section is
    ALWAYS present — a balanced run needs its verdict line too. The agreement with the real
    process exit code is the CLI's own scenario, not this one's.

    @unit @SCN-RPT-022
    Scenario: the summary opens with the run verdict and the exit code it maps to

  Rule: a proof that never ran is attributed to no scenario, even when it cites one

    The census's second lie, and the reason the fold is not a no-op. A `broken` observation
    can carry a REAL `scnId` — an ambiguous cucumber step cites its @SCN and
    still never runs. The census counted it `attributed`; the reconciler posts it to suspense.
    A proof that never ran cannot discharge or fail an obligation, so it
    attributes to nothing.

    The count moves — `attributed` down, `suspense` up — and this scenario NAMES that move,
    because nothing else can. The exhaustion identity is INVARIANT under a misclassification
    between its own terms: it catches an evidence observation VANISHING, never an evidence observation in the wrong bucket.
    It is a completeness check, and it has been misread as a correctness check. This is the
    correctness check.

    The census is now a FOLD over the Ledger: it walks the posted evidence observations and reads each
    evidence observation's kind off the ROW it posted to, so the reconciler is the ONE classifier. The fold
    keeps the BEHAVIOURAL scoping, and citation-routing makes that scoping MORE obviously
    necessary rather than less. A fitness evidence observation now cites its OWN fitness
    scenario's @SCN and posts to an ordinary scenario row — so to a naive walk it looks
    EXACTLY like a behavioural evidence observation, indistinguishable by the account it landed on. The fold
    must therefore skip it on the CHECK (`qualityCheck === "fitness"`), never on the absence of
    a citation. Under the retired name-match posting a fitness evidence observation cited nothing at all,
    so a walk keyed on "does it cite?" would have got the right answer for the wrong reason;
    that reading is now simply wrong, and the fitness-function axis the census must not see at all
    would be counted into the Test volume.

    @unit @SCN-RPT-023
    Scenario: a broken observation citing a real scenario counts as suspense, not attributed to that scenario

  Rule: the reconciliation collection renders as "Scenario Reconciliation(s)", never "scenario(s)", on every surface that names it

    The vocabulary rule for the reconciled OUTPUT collection (doc/ubiquitous-language.md,
    Scenario Reconciliation: "scenarios" is on its Double-check line — it holds Scenario
    Reconciliations, not scenarios). The report
    names the collection on two rendered surfaces and both must read the same word: the Subject
    CELL of the reconciliation-collection row, and the headline verdict tally under
    "## Scenario Reconciliations". The tally counts EVERY row — real, phantom and suspense
    — so it names Scenario Reconciliations, not scenarios. A term owned on
    one surface but not the other is exactly how the tally drifted to "scenario(s)" while the
    Subject cell and the field names stayed right. Asserts the rendered CELL VALUE and
    the tally STRING, never their section headings — the heading is a separate concern.

    @unit @SCN-RPT-024
    Scenario: the reconciliation collection is named "Scenario Reconciliation(s)" on every surface, never "scenario(s)"

  Rule: the tool identity is the tool's OWN checkout — an installed tool has none, whatever repository it is sitting inside

    Which spec-controller produced a run.
    The tool identity answers "which tool is this?" from the tool's OWN install, never the
    target. Run from a checkout of itself the tool SHA is that checkout's HEAD; resolved out of
    a node_modules tree the tool has no checkout of its own and the SHA resolves to a recorded
    null — while the version, read from the tool's own package.json, is STILL present, so a run
    stays attributable by version even with no SHA.

    TWO TRAPS, AND THE SECOND IS WHY THIS RULE GAINED A SCENARIO. The first is cwd: at
    run time cwd is the TARGET repo, so a bare `git rev-parse HEAD` would re-record the target's
    SHA, and reading `git -C <the tool's own dir>` (derived from import.meta.url) is what closes
    it. The second is that the tool's own dir is ITSELF inside the target — the ordinary installed
    case, <consumer>/node_modules/…, and the only one a consumer ever sees. `git -C` walks up to
    the consumer's .git and answers with the consumer's HEAD; being .gitignored makes no
    difference, git does not stop at ignored directories, and nothing on the rendered report
    distinguishes that answer from a genuine tool SHA. The `-C` guard could not see it, which is
    why the node_modules segment is answered BEFORE git is asked at all.

    THE TRADE THE SECOND GUARD MUST NOT MAKE. A tool reached through a node_modules symlink to a
    checkout of itself is still a checkout of itself: Node resolves module symlinks, so the dir it
    reports carries no node_modules segment and keeps its real SHA. Answering null everywhere
    would trade one wrong SHA for another and nothing on the report would say which, so that arm
    is asserted positively rather than left to follow.

    RPT-026 is a pure resolution rule proven in isolation by Vitest. RPT-029 is NOT, and that is the whole of its reason: this fault was invisible to
    every projection over a hand-built run context, so its integration arm stands where a consumer
    stands — the packed tarballs really installed, inside a real git repository, the real command
    run there, the rendered line read back.

    @unit @SCN-RPT-026
    Scenario: the tool names its own checkout's commit, and where it is not a git checkout names no commit and still names its version

    @unit @integration @SCN-RPT-029
    Scenario: a spec-controller resolved out of node_modules reports no tool SHA though the tree it sits in is a git repository, while one running from a checkout of itself still reports that checkout's HEAD
      Given this release's packages installed into a directory that is a git repository, into a directory that is not one, and reached from a third through a node_modules symlink to a checkout of the tool
      When each one runs a reconcile and the provenance it renders is read
      Then the one installed inside a git repository reports no tool SHA, and does not name that repository's commit
      And the one installed outside any repository reports no tool SHA
      And the one reached from a checkout of the tool reports that checkout's commit, not the consumer's
      And all three still report the tool's version

  Rule: the Run provenance names every stream the run was handed, and every one it was not

    What was this verdict computed from. The canonical input
    list the Inputs block renders carried five flags, and the fitness stream was not among
    them — so a report whose every configured fitness row was answered from that stream did
    not admit reading it. The stream is OPTIONAL by design, passed only where the file
    exists, so its absence from the block was genuinely ambiguous between "the run never got
    it" and "the run got it and the block does not say so" — the two readings the Inputs block
    exists to keep apart.

    THE CANONICAL LIST GAINS THE STREAM AND NOTHING ELSE MOVES. It is appended after
    --package-json, so no line that was already rendered changes position; the presence
    filter is untouched, so a run not handed it still renders exactly the flags it was
    handed and its JSON provenance.inputs object serialises byte-identically. Both surfaces
    project the SAME ctx.inputs — the object the reconcile itself consumed — so the block is
    never a second opinion about what was read.

    @unit @SCN-RPT-027
    Scenario: the Inputs block names the optional fitness stream when the run received it

    @unit @SCN-RPT-028
    Scenario: a canonical input the run did not receive is named as absent rather than omitted, so silence is never the answer
      Silence is never the answer. Naming every stream a run received removed the "the run
      got it and the block does not say so" reading. It
      did NOT answer "was the fitness stream consulted?" for a reader who does not already hold
      the canonical list in their head — absence from a list is only informative to someone
      who knows what the list could have contained. So the block names the canonical inputs
      with no value too, on a line of their own.

      A SEPARATE LINE, NOT AN INTERLEAVED MARKER, AND THE REASON IS A BRANCH IT WOULD HAVE
      KILLED. Threading absent entries through the Inputs list makes the "none specified"
      branch unreachable and silently rewrites a rendering that already exists. A separate
      line leaves the list, its order and its empty branch byte-identical, and costs one line.
      It renders only when something IS absent: a "none supplied" line on the healthy path
      would be noise in a block whose whole discipline is facts only.

      THE FACT IS DERIVED, NOT PERSISTED. The JSON provenance object gains nothing here.
      The provenance follows the project's rule — persist a fact on JSON iff it is not
      derivable — and absence is exactly derivable, from the inputs object plus the canonical
      flag list. A second copy would agree with the first until one of them was edited.

  Rule: an evidence observation that answers no evidence obligation shows its own result

    A failing test is still a failing test when it answers no evidence obligation. On a phantom
    row, and on a scenario's row when the scenario holds no obligation at the check it ran at,
    the report lists each such observation by its outcome and where it ran; were it to drop the
    result the observation holds, a failure would read exactly like a pass. The outcome, its
    glyph and its sentence stay as they are, and the observation's own result is added beside
    them. The suspense row counts its results by file instead, under the Rule below.

